Overlay patch 0027 - async Metal shader compilation.

CLASS: (a) program-baseline parity.

This is `docs/lus-divergence.md` row 0035, the last unported item of the
SoH port's LUS perf set that can stall the game thread for longer than the audio
reservoir holds. The SoH port's patch (`Shipwright-ios/overlay/patches/
0035-lus-metal-async-shaders.patch`, its D-034) came from 2ship's device-validated
finding (`FINDINGS-FROM-2SHIP-async-shader-stutter.md`): a cold `newLibrary` is
**120-160 ms per shader** on A-series silicon, `newRenderPipelineState` another
~25 %, and this fork does both **synchronously, inside the draw path**
(`GfxRenderingAPIMetal::CreateAndLoadNewShader`, called from the interpreter the
first time a colour-combiner/geometry-mode pair is seen).

WHY IT IS IN THIS ROUND
-----------------------
Round 13 is the ~5 s audio/video stutter (the design notes D26). `docs/frame-map.md`:
the game thread wakes the audio thread once per frame and then blocks on it, and
the reservoir is 52.5 ms (90 ms after overlay 0026). ANY game-thread stall past
that is heard as a click and seen as a freeze, together. A 150 ms synchronous
shader compile is three times the old budget in one call.

The simulator's attract demo does NOT reproduce this half - overlay 0012 rev2's
`lib_n`/`pso_n` read 0 across every sampled window (the measurement log M-025), because
the demo revisits scenes whose shaders are already in the pool. The user's device
session was real play, moving through new rooms, which is exactly when a new
combiner pair appears - and the device log's median gap between underrun
clusters (4.06 s) is the shape of "a new shader every few seconds while
exploring". So this patch ships on the strength of the SoH port's device
validation and the mechanism, and is labelled honestly in MEASUREMENTS as
*not* measured on the simulator; `lib_ms`/`pso_ms` are in the `fps` line so the
device round can settle it in one reading.

HOW - REV 2, THE JOB OWNS ITS OUTPUTS
-------------------------------------
Register the program shell and everything the draw path reads SYNCHRONOUSLY -
the interpreter binds the returned pointer immediately - and move only
`newLibrary` + `newRenderPipelineState` onto a detached worker.

Rev 1 handed the worker a raw `ShaderProgramMetal*` into `mShaderProgramPool`
and let it write `pipeline_state_variants[...]` directly. **That was a
use-after-free**, reachable in ordinary play: `Interpreter::SetShaderSettingValue`
(interpreter.cpp:5357, every shader-setting CVar) calls `gfx_shader_cache_clear()`
-> `ClearShaderCache()`, which marks EVERY pool entry `markedForDeletion`, and
the next `LookupShader()` for that key **erases** the entry - while the worker
still holds the pointer. Rev 1's header note argued no marked program could be
in flight; that is simply false once a cache clear can arrive from the menu
mid-compile. Round 14's review caught it before any device build shipped.

Rev 2 makes the compile job an owner, not a borrower:

* `PBIosShaderCompileJob` (gfx_metal.cpp, `Fast` namespace) is a
  `std::shared_ptr` struct holding *everything* the worker needs (a RETAINED
  `MTL::Device*`, a retained `MTL::VertexDescriptor*`, the MSL source, the
  alpha flag, the enabled MSAA levels) and *everything* it produces (its own
  `variants[]` array) plus an `std::atomic<int> state`
  (pending / ready / failed).
* **The worker never touches the pool entry** - no `this`, no `prg`, no
  `mDevice`, no `mMsaaNumQualityLevels`. It fills `job->variants[]` and then
  publishes with ONE release store of `state`.
* **The render thread delivers.** `PBIosClaimShaderJob(prg)` runs in
  `LookupShader` and in `DrawTriangles` (the only two places that look at a
  program's pipelines): it acquire-loads `state`, and on `ready` MOVES each
  pointer out of the job into `prg->pipeline_state_variants[...]`, then drops
  the entry's reference. Release/acquire, properly paired - rev 1 had a bare
  `atomic_thread_fence(release)` on the writer and nothing at all on the reader.
* **Erase is now safe.** The entry holds only a `shared_ptr`; erasing it (or
  overwriting it with a newer job for the same key) drops one reference. The
  worker's own copy keeps the job alive, it finishes into its own struct, and
  the last reference to die releases the device, the vertex descriptor and any
  pipeline states nobody claimed. `ClearShaderCache` needs no change and no
  caveat.
* The partial-failure path (library compiled, `newFunction` returned null)
  releases BOTH functions, not neither, and marks the job failed so the entry
  stops waiting for it.

The synchronous path (`gSohIos.AsyncShaders 0`) runs the very same job function
inline and claims it immediately, so there is one code path to reason about and
the variants are non-null by the time `CreateAndLoadNewShader` returns.

The draw path still has to tolerate a null pipeline for a few frames:
`DrawTriangles` returns early WITHOUT advancing `mLastShaderProgram` (so the
bind is retried next draw) and WITHOUT advancing `mCurrentVertexBufferOffset`
(the slot is reused). The geometry pops in a frame or two later instead of the
whole frame stalling.

`gSohIos.AsyncShaders`, default **ON**, live kill switch over the console bridge
(`set gSohIos.AsyncShaders 0`) and a checkbox in Settings > iOS > Advanced
(overlay 0021). __IOS__-only: the macOS oracle keeps the pristine synchronous
path, which is what makes it an oracle.

WHERE THIS FORK DIVERGES FROM THE SOH PORT'S 0035
--------------------------------------------------
Recorded because the upstream-bump drill needs it:

1. **No `mSrgbMode`.** This fork hardwires `MTL::PixelFormatBGRA8Unorm` for the
   colour attachment; the SoH port's LUS picks `_sRGB` from a member. One less
   captured variable.
2. **More program fields.** This fork's `ShaderProgramMetal` carries
   `usedTextures[2..5]` (masks and blend), `usedTextures[SHADER_PALETTE_TEXTURE]`
   and `usedLighting`, which the SoH port's does not. All are set synchronously
   here, because `DrawTriangles` reads them before it ever looks at the pipeline.
3. **`LookupShader` can erase - and this patch survives it.** The SoH port's
   0035 (and this patch's rev 1) hand the worker a raw pool pointer and argue
   the erase cannot race; see above, it can. The ownership model here is the
   fix, and it is the one worth sending upstream and back to the siblings.
   It costs one `shared_ptr` per `ShaderProgramMetal` (the only header change).
4. **The error path returns nullptr upstream.** Upstream bails out of
   `CreateAndLoadNewShader` when `newLibrary` fails. Asynchronously there is
   nobody to return to, so a failed library logs, marks the job failed and
   leaves the variants null - which the draw guard already handles as "not
   ready", i.e. that shader's geometry never draws instead of crashing on a
   null pipeline state. That is strictly better than upstream's synchronous
   path, which returns nullptr to an interpreter that dereferences it (overlay
   0002's original crash).

UPSTREAMABLE: yes, as an iOS/mobile option - it is the same patch three siblings
already carry, plus the ownership fix they all still need.

